  "the_finding_this_supports": "removing an IAM permission is not an instantaneous remedy, and confirming the denial does not establish that it has converged. The document's route-4 and section-4 remedies read as though revoking a grant closes the path at once; measured here, invocations kept succeeding after DeleteRolePolicy returned 200 AND after three consecutive denials were observed. Amendment candidate \u2014 see results/FINDING-F5-1-REVOCATION.md.",
  "what_this_actually_measured": "20 direct lambda:Invoke attempts sent after DeleteRolePolicy returned successfully, of which 9 still EXECUTED and 11 were denied. The denial had NOT been confirmed to hold when they were sent \u2014 see why_moved.",
  "why_moved": "`restored_reassert` is defined as invocations AFTER the denial has been re-asserted, and 'after' was not established when these were sent. `_wait_for_effect` ended the revoke wait on a SINGLE `denied_by_iam` probe after the sequence `executed x5 -> denied_by_iam`, and that first denial was a flap in a fleet that had not converged, so 'after the denial was re-asserted' was never established. The wait now requires PROP_CONFIRM_N consecutive confirmations."
